          "question": "I can only get a few words out before I have to stop for air, and my rescue puffer isn't doing anything tonight.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now. Do not wait.** Ask someone nearby to help you, or have them take you to the nearest emergency department. Do not drive yourself.\n\nBeing able to say only a few words before you must stop for air, while your rescue inhaler is not helping, is a warning sign of a severe asthma attack. The red-flag guidance on severe breathing difficulty says that when too little air moves, a quiet chest can mean the airways have worsened, not improved (source: \"Severe difficulty breathing,\" emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance). Our triage tool returned a lower tier because it did not match this exact wording. I am overriding that result because the risk of missing a severe attack is too high.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
